 Accordingly, this is an optimizer study on a SimBench-derived mixed-voltage portfolio proxy, not an action-aligned distribution expansion study. The candidate attributes are generated from subnet statistics, and the AC stage maps portfolio compositions onto separate networks rather than evaluating the optimizer's selected actions at their original nodes.
 
 The contributions of this paper are:
 
 1. **A constraint-aware multi-objective DE framework with testable mechanism groups on a portfolio proxy.** CARS-MODE integrates jDE parameter control, a success-driven two-strategy pool, deterministic budget repair, and crowding diversity. Repair and diversity are removed individually; a combined control replaces adaptive parameters and the strategy pool with fixed $F/CR$ and rand/1. The design therefore separates the framework-level proxy result from repair and diversity effects while testing parameter-and-strategy adaptation as a bundle (Section 4).
-2. **A reproducible public mixed-voltage portfolio-proxy benchmark with a method-independent evaluation protocol.** Seven deterministic planning experiments (candidate-pool, load-factor, and budget variants) over at most 72 candidate actions derived from SimBench subnet statistics; standard hypervolume under fixed seeded normalization bounds; 30 seeds per stochastic method and experiment (Sections 2 and 5). The benchmark does not provide nodal, monetarily calibrated expansion actions.
-3. **A statistically grounded proxy-front result against direct controls.** CARS-MODE attains pooled mean hypervolume 0.04218, 6.22% above NSGA-II with returned-population repair (0.03971), and records 55 significant wins among 56 stochastic-baseline comparisons. Seven comparisons with the deterministic Weighted Sum rule are descriptive and favor CARS-MODE in mean HV. Its margins over GDE3 and NSDE are 8.57% and 8.52%, with significant separation in every planning experiment; these are hypervolume results on the proxy, not evidence of superior expansion actions (Sections 6.1 and 6.6).
-4. **A two-level component and validity analysis.** Budget repair (−6.84% when removed) and diversity preservation (−33.56%) carry the proxy gain, whereas the combined parameter-and-strategy adaptation bundle is unresolved (FixedDE +0.60% in pooled mean). The pandapower composition check identifies a proxy--physics disagreement and a descriptive full-versus-FixedDE difference (0.611 versus 0.569 overall AC feasibility), but it does not overturn the unresolved proxy contrast or identify either adaptive subcomponent as the causal source (Sections 6.2–6.3).
+2. **A reproducible public mixed-voltage portfolio-proxy benchmark with an auditable evaluation protocol.** Seven deterministic planning experiments over at most 72 SimBench-derived candidate actions; 30 seeds per stochastic method and experiment; primary standard hypervolume under fixed seeded bounds and clipping; and a separate 1050-run core audit under analytic feasible bounds, a tighter reference point, and common-reference IGD+ (Sections 2, 5, and 6.7). The benchmark does not provide nodal, monetarily calibrated expansion actions.
+3. **A statistically grounded but metric-conditional proxy-front result.** Under the prespecified primary hypervolume configuration, CARS-MODE attains 0.04218, 6.22% above NSGA-II with returned-population repair (0.03971), and records 55 significant wins among 56 stochastic-baseline comparisons. Its primary margins over GDE3 and NSDE are 8.57% and 8.52%, with significant separation in every planning experiment. The analytic-bound and common-reference diagnostics do not preserve the CARS-MODE-versus-NSGA ranking, so these are primary-configuration proxy results rather than normalization-robust evidence (Sections 6.1, 6.6, and 6.7).
+4. **A bounded component and physical-validity analysis.** Budget repair (−6.84% when removed) and diversity preservation (−33.56%) carry the primary proxy gain, whereas the combined parameter-and-strategy adaptation bundle is unresolved (FixedDE +0.60% in pooled mean). The pandapower check covers 11 methods and identifies a descriptive full-versus-FixedDE difference (0.611 versus 0.569 overall AC feasibility), but its three seed-0 compositions per method support neither hierarchical uncertainty nor component causality (Sections 6.2–6.3).
 
