+The new core rerun first checks the primary indicator mechanically. Across 40,933 feasible front points, the largest sampled-normalized coordinate is 0.95455, so the reference point 1.1 dominates every point even before clipping. No coordinate exceeds 1.0. The lower side is different: 1377 coordinates fall below zero, affecting 594 of 1050 run fronts. Clipping therefore truncates better-than-bound performance rather than rescuing reference-point dominance; clipped HV is on average 0.00121 lower than its unclipped counterpart, with an experiment-level maximum absolute change of 0.00514. Under the analytic bounds, every normalized coordinate lies in \([0,1]\), so both 1.1 and the tighter 1.05 dominate without clipping.
